WEBVTT

NOTE Narration by an AI voice: Matilda (XrExE9yKIg1WjnnlVkGX), eleven_v4.

intro
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:10.300
A password-spray alert has been quiet for weeks. Here GitHub Copilot CLI hunts for a slow spray with Splunk and the Machine Data Lake.

ask
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:18.640
The hunter asks Copilot to check the last fourteen days of sign-ins and name the source that looks like a spray.

searches
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:28.920
Copilot searches the hot index through Splunk MCP: the fields first, then failures per source, then the busiest five minutes.

why-quiet
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.480
That five-minute check matters. A spray that tries each account once never trips an alert on a hundred failures.

suspect
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:47.600
One address stands out, with about five hundred failures across three hundred accounts, never more than one in five minutes.

vpn
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:54.940
The busy VPN fails too, but mostly succeeds. That's the false positive to avoid.

notebook
00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:03.860
Copilot opens the notebook and sets the suspect IP. Lake searches cost more, so it asks first.

lake-approved
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:11.800
Approved, the raw search reads this address's full history, a hundred and twenty days.

history
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:18.720
The index saw ten days. The lake shows a September campaign, then a return.

table-ask
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.920
Loading the shared table needs approval too.

table-approved
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:33.120
Copilot loads the ninety-day analytics table into pandas through Open Sharing, with no export or copy.

table-why
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:40.460
The lake promoted those days into a five-column table, so pandas reads only the fields the hunt needs.

table-result
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:47.700
A hundred and four thousand sign-ins over ninety days. The backtest needs your go-ahead too.

backtest-approved
00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:55.820
The backtest runs the new rule over every sign-in in the kernel. Nothing is written to Splunk.

rule
00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:05.780
It flags only the suspect, on thirteen days. The old alert would never have fired, and the busy VPN is skipped.

intel
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:13.520
It's in a community threat feed, and it got into a privileged backup account at three in the morning, two days ago.

finding
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:23.180
Copilot sums up the case and next steps: reset that account, block the address, review its activity.

traceable
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:30.840
Every claim traces back to a notebook cell, so the hunt is reproducible and easy to review.

record
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:40.640
The executed notebook is the record: every query, table, and chart, ending in the daily search to save as the alert.

value-data
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:48.780
Splunk keeps fourteen hot days fast, and the Machine Data Lake keeps months of history cheaply.

value-agent
00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:57.880
An agent turns that history into a tested detection in minutes, with a person approving every costly step.
